.TH lpr "" "Command"
.SH NAME
lpr \- send to line printer spooler
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlpr [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
lpr -- send to line printer spooler
Usage:	lpr [ options ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-b	Next argument is the banner
	-c	Make a copy of each 'file' in spool area
	-m	Send a message when listing is complete
	-n	No message (default)
	-r	Remove files when they have been spooled
.HE
.B lpr
spools each named
.I file
for printing on the line printer.
If no
.I file
is named, it spools what it reads from the standard input.
For each file,
.B lpr
writes a control-file entry and, unless the file is read directly from
its home directory, a copy of the data into the spool directory,
.BR /usr/spool/lpd ,
and then starts the line-printer daemon,
.BR /usr/lib/lpd .
.IP "\fB\-b \fIbanner\fR"
Use
.I banner
as one more line of the banner page, instead of the default,
which is the user's login name.
Up to two
.B \-b
banners may be given (the first four banner lines are already used
for the Coherent logo, the date, and the user name).
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Copy each
.I file
into the spooling directory instead of printing it from its own
location, so the file can be changed after it is queued.
This is implied by
.BR \-r .
.IP \fB\-m\fR
Write a message on the user's terminal when printing completes.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Do not send a completion message (the default).
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Remove each
.I file
once it has been copied into the spool area (implies
.BR \-c ).
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/usr/spool/lpd\fR	spool directory
\fB/usr/lib/lpd\fR	line printer daemon
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B lpskip
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B lpr
reports and removes any temporary spool files it created if it cannot
open a named file, cannot create its control or data file, or cannot
find the daemon
.BR /usr/lib/lpd .
